From: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
Date: Tue, 03 Nov 2026 00:00:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: allow slab-backed RX buffers independently

A receive buffer retained by a hardware ring pins its shared page-fragment
allocation even after the other fragments have been freed. Sparse buffer
replacement across several radios can therefore retain hundreds of MiB
of mostly unused pages.

Allow boards to select the existing slab-backed allocator independently
of the low-memory host profile. Preserve ring sizes, descriptor counts,
firmware memory mode, DMA ownership and the default allocator elsewhere.

Signed-off-by: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
@@ -1894,6 +1894,11 @@
 	const struct ath12k_mem_profile_based_param *param;
 
 	param = &ath12k_mem_profile_based_param[ab->target_mem_mode];
+	/* Long-lived RX ring entries can pin mostly unused shared page fragments.
+	 * Allow slab-backed buffers without reducing host or firmware capacity.
+	 */
+	ab->low_memory_rx = of_property_read_bool(ab->dev->of_node,
+						"qcom,rx-skb-slab");
 	/* An integrated radio can have a fixed default-mode firmware layout
 	 * while sharing a small host with a discrete radio. Only reduce host
 	 * rings and descriptor pools; leave QMI and WMI capacity unchanged.
